# 2. Build natively on the *new* Letta, not legacy Letta

This is important because Letta has changed direction substantially.

For new projects Letta now recommends its **TypeScript Agent SDK**, which exposes the same interface across local execution, Letta Cloud, and a self-hosted App Server. A persistent agent can be created once and resumed elsewhere while preserving memory/state. ([GitHub][1])

Meanwhile Letta is explicitly retiring older server-centric abstractions such as legacy memory tools, server templates, hard-coded multi-agent APIs and server-side MCP patterns in favor of:

* Agent SDK;
* computer use;
* MemFS/context repositories;
* skills;
* subagents;
* client-side/runtime-native tooling. ([Letta][2])

So don't deeply integrate `mw` with old `letta-client` Python models.

### Add one small TypeScript service

```text
services/
  runtime-letta/
    src/
      adapter.ts
      sessions.ts
      memory.ts
      trajectory.ts
      learning.ts
      tools.ts
```

Use:

```text
@letta-ai/letta-agent-sdk
@letta-ai/trajectory
```

There currently isn't a Python Agent SDK, so this TS boundary is cleaner than trying to talk directly to internal Letta protocols from Python. ([GitHub][3])

Keep:

```text
Oracle       Python
WorkerKit    Python
Market       Python
cg           Python
Lab control  Python
```

and communicate with `runtime-letta` through a tiny internal HTTP/WebSocket protocol.

