# 34. Phala/dstack belongs below all of this

dstack is perfectly suited because it accepts normal Docker Compose workloads and provides encrypted execution, workload identity, attestation and TEE-derived per-app keys. ([GitHub][11])

Mount:

```text
/var/run/dstack.sock
```

only where required.

WorkerKit asks dstack:

```text
getKey("/moltwork/worker/<worker-id>/signing/v1")
```

to derive a signing key.

dstack supports deterministic key derivation plus custom report data in its attestation quote. ([GitHub][12])

---

# 35. What exactly should be attested

Do not attest:

> “this agent is good.”

Attest execution identity.

Before Run:

```text
worker_version_digest
work_order_digest
process_version_digest
runtime_image_digest
```

During/after:

```text
event_chain_head
artifact_root
cost_summary_digest
```

Create:

```text
RunCommitment =
SHA256(
  WorkerVersion
  || WorkOrder
  || ProcessVersion
  || event_chain_head
  || artifact_root
)
```

Request:

```text
dstack.getQuote(RunCommitment)
```

Store quote in WorkReceipt.

Now a third party can verify:

> this exact measured environment executed this exact Worker configuration and committed to this run evidence.

Not whether the answer was brilliant.

---

# 36. TEE keys should belong to the Moltwork Worker

Conceptually:

```text
Worker-7
    ↓
dstack derived signing key
    ↓
public Worker identity
```

Then every WorkReceipt gets signed.

If it moves to another authorized dstack deployment, your key/identity policy can determine whether it retains or rotates execution identity.

This is where later ERC-8004 identity can bind.

---

# 37. Keep inference outside the TEE initially if necessary

You can still attest WorkerKit/Letta execution while calling external inference APIs.

But be precise:

```text
TEE protects:
Worker state
memory
secrets
business logic
event ledger

External provider sees:
prompts sent to it
```

If full confidentiality is required later, dstack explicitly supports architectures where the agent and LLM inference endpoint both run in confidential environments. ([GitHub][13])

Don't block v1 on that.

